titute, Istanbul Department, İstanbul, Turkey"}]},"aff0005":{"#name":"affiliation","$":{"id":"aff0005","affiliation-id":"S1296207421001096-579ab7459b127a8fdfd93f189e5cba90"},"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"e"},{"#name":"textfn","$":{"id":"cetextfn0005"},"_":"Konya Technical University, Faculty of Engineering and Natural Sciences, Department of Geological Engineering, Konya, Turkey"},{"#name":"affiliation","$":{"xmlns:sa":true},"$$":[{"#name":"organization","_":"Konya Technical University"},{"#name":"organization","_":"Faculty of Engineering and Natural Sciences"},{"#name":"organization","_":"Department of Geological Engineering"},{"#name":"city","_":"Konya"},{"#name":"country","_":"Turkey"}]},{"#name":"source-text","$":{"id":"staff0005"},"_":"eKonya Technical University, Faculty of Engineering and Natural Sciences, Department of Geological Engineering, Konya, Turkey"}]}},"correspondences":{"cor0001":{"#name":"correspondence","$":{"id":"cor0001"},"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"⁎"},{"#name":"text","$":{"id":"cetext0001"},"_":"Corresponding author."}]}},"attachments":[],"scopusAuthorIds":{},"articles":{}},"authorMetadata":[],"banner":{"expanded":false},"biographies":{},"body":{},"chapters":{"toc":[],"isLoading":false},"changeViewLinks":{"showFullTextLink":true,"showAbstractLink":false},"citingArticles":{"hitCount":33,"viewMoreUrl":"http://www.scopus.com/scopus/inward/citedby.url?partnerID=10&rel=3.0.0&eid=2-s2.0-85111321583&md5=5cd57b61fda363314c38048fa69e3a","articles":[{"articleTitle":"Artificial intelligence-assisted visual inspection for cultural heritage: State-of-the-art review","authors":"Mishra M., Lourenço P.B.","doi":"10.1016/j.culher.2024.01.005","externalArticle":false,"issn":"12962074","openAccess":1,"page":"536-550","pii":"S1296207424000050","publicationDate":"2024-03-01","publicationTitle":"Journal of Cultural Heritage","publicationYear":"2024","scopusArticleUrl":"http://www.scopus.com/scopus/inward/record.url?partnerID=10&rel=3.0.0&view=basic&eid=2-s2.0-85185186401&md5=f2d64babdf051db6e5846c8a0cb1f69","scopusEid":"2-s2.0-85185186401","thirdParty":false,"volume":"Volume 66","abstract":{"$$":[{"$$":[{"$":{"id":"sctt0001"},"#name":"section-title","_":"Highlights"},{"$$":[{"$$":[{"$$":[{"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"•"},{"$":{"view":"all","id":"p0001"},"#name":"para","_":"The state-of-the-art research on artificial intelligence assisted visual inspection systems for CH has been reviewed."}],"$":{"id":"lstitem0001"},"#name":"list-item"},{"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"•"},{"$":{"view":"all","id":"p0002"},"#name":"para","_":"Several case studies on AI-applications to assists manual visual inspections of CH are highlighted."}],"$":{"id":"lstitem0002"},"#name":"list-item"},{"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"•"},{"$":{"view":"all","id":"p0003"},"#name":"para","_":"This review is intended to broaden future studies and developments in this field."}],"$":{"id":"lstitem0003"},"#name":"list-item"},{"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"•"},{"$":{"view":"all","id":"p0004"},"#name":"para","_":"A great potential exists in this field of AI-assisted visual inspections combining drones and IoT technologies."}],"$":{"id":"lstitem0004"},"#name":"list-item"}],"$":{"id":"lst0001"},"#name":"list"}],"$":{"view":"all","id":"sp0001"},"#name":"simple-para"}],"$":{"view":"all","id":"abssec0001"},"#name":"abstract-sec"}],"$":{"view":"all","id":"absh001","class":"author-highlights"},"#name":"abstract"},{"$$":[{"$":{"id":"sctt0002"},"#name":"section-title","_":"Abstract"},{"$$":[{"$":{"view":"all","id":"sp0002"},"#name":"simple-para","_":"Applying computer science techniques such as artificial intelligence (AI), deep learning (DL), and computer vision (CV) on digital image data can help monitor and preserve cultural heritage (CH) sites. Defects such as weathering, removal of mortar, joint damage, discoloration, erosion, surface cracks, vegetation, seepage, and vandalism and their propagation with time adversely affect the structural health of CH sites. Several studies have reported damage detection in concrete and bridge structures using AI techniques. However, few studies have quantified defects in CH structures using the AI paradigm, and limited case studies exist for their applications. Hence, the application of AI-assisted visual inspections for CH sites needs to be explored. AI-assisted digital inspections assist inspection professionals and increase confidence levels in the damage assessment of CH buildings. This review summarizes the damage assessment techniques using image processing techniques, focusing mainly on DL techniques applied for CH conservation. Several case study applications of CH buildings are presented where AI can assist in traditional visual inspections."}],"$":{"view":"all","id":"abssec0002"},"#name":"abstract-sec"}],"$":{"view":"all","id":"abs0001","class":"author"},"#name":"abstract"}],"$":{"xmlns:ce":true,"xmlns:dm":true,"xmlns:sb":true},"#name":"abstracts"}},{"articleTitle":"Preserving our heritage: A photogrammetry-based digital twin framework for monitoring deteriorations of historic structures","authors":"Kong X., Hucks R.G.","doi":"10.1016/j.autcon.2023.104928","externalArticle":false,"issn":"09265805","openAccess":0,"pii":"S0926580523001887","publicationDate":"2023-08-01","publicationTitle":"Automation in Construction","publicationYear":"2023","scopusArticleUrl":"http://www.scopus.com/scopus/inward/record.url?partnerID=10&rel=3.0.0&view=basic&eid=2-s2.0-85162832761&md5=72cd26546444c2da83fb52ceb9f95f13","scopusEid":"2-s2.0-85162832761","snippets":["Pathak et al. [15] trained a convolution neural network to classify spalling and cracks from an image dataset of a historic temple in Thailand. Hatır al [16] leveraged R-CNN to detect multiple types of stone deteriorations and further mapped them back to orthophotos. For a comprehensive review of recent advances in machine learning-based methods in SHM of historic structures, the readers are referred to [17]."],"thirdParty":false,"volume":"Volume 152","abstract":{"$$":[{"$$":[{"$":{"id":"st0005"},"#name":"section-title","_":"Abstract"},{"$$":[{"$$":[{"#name":"__text__","_":"Preservation of historic structures is a global challenge for governments and site owners, as accumulated deteriorations could weaken the structural integrity. Recent advances in the digital twin in civil and infrastructure engineering have shown great promise in creating virtual models of a physical entity under time-varying physical states. Because of this unique feature, assessing time-varying structural deteriorations become feasible. This paper presents a novel methodological attempt of health monitoring for historic structures by integrating photogrammetry technologies and point cloud processing algorithms into a digital twin framework. To make this viable, virtual models of the physical structure at different inspection times are first created "},{"#name":"italic","_":"via"},{"#name":"__text__","_":" photogrammetry technologies. Then, a bridge health monitoring method is established to align two virtual models together and further differentiate the changes provoked by structural deterioration. We validate the proposed methodology in a historic stone arch bridge for detecting multiple simulated structural deteriorations."}],"$":{"view":"all","id":"sp0115"},"#name":"simple-para"}],"$":{"view":"all","id":"as0005"},"#name":"abstract-sec"}],"$":{"view":"all","id":"ab0005","lang":"en","class":"author"},"#name":"abstract"},{"$$":[{"$":{"id":"st0010"},"#name":"section-title","_":"Highlights"},{"$$":[{"$$":[{"$$":[{"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"•"},{"$$":[{"#name":"__text__","_":"Building high-fidelity virtual models of a stone arch historic bridge "},{"#name":"italic","_":"via"},{"#name":"__text__","_":" SfM-MVS."}],"$":{"view":"all","id":"p0005"},"#name":"para"}],"$":{"id":"li0005"},"#name":"list-item"},{"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"•"},{"$":{"view":"all","id":"p0010"},"#name":"para","_":"Detecting structural damage at small and large scales, or any scales in between."}],"$":{"id":"li0010"},"#name":"list-item"},{"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"•"},{"$":{"view":"all","id":"p0015"},"#name":"para","_":"Adaptable for cultural heritage communities that have limited resources."}],"$":{"id":"li0015"},"#name":"list-item"},{"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"•"},{"$":{"view":"all","id":"p0020"},"#name":"para","_":"Validated through monitoring loosened stones, cracks, and uneven settlements."}],"$":{"id":"li0020"},"#name":"list-item"}],"$":{"id":"l0005"},"#name":"list"}],"$":{"view":"all","id":"sp0120"},"#name":"simple-para"}],"$":{"view":"all","id":"as0010"},"#name":"abstract-sec"}],"$":{"view":"all","id":"ab0010","lang":"en","class":"author-highlights"},"#name":"abstract"}],"$":{"xmlns:ce":true,"xmlns:dm":true,"xmlns:sb":true},"#name":"abstracts"}},{"articleTitle":"Deep Learning-Based Automated Detection of Cracks in Historical Masonry Structures","authors":"Hacıefendioğlu K., Altunışık A.C., Abdioğlu T.","doi":"10.3390/buildings13123113","externalArticle":true,"openAccess":0,"publicationDate":"December 2023","publicationTitle":"Buildings","publicationYear":"2023","scopusArticleUrl":"http://www.scopus.com/scopus/inward/record.url?partnerID=10&rel=3.0.0&view=basic&eid=2-s2.0-85180553560&md5=aa47e68afcb7b716532f1b68ae8c2cc7","scopusEid":"2-s2.0-85180553560","thirdParty":false,"volume":"Volume 13","abstract":{}},{"articleTitle":"Reviewing and Integrating AEC Practices into Industry 6.0: Strategies for Smart and Sustainable Future-Built Environments","authors":"Almusaed A., Yitmen I., Almssad A.","doi":"10.3390/su151813464","externalArticle":true,"openAccess":0,"publicationDate":"September 2023","publicationTitle":"Sustainability (Switzerland)","publicationYear":"2023","scopusArticleUrl":"http://www.scopus.com/scopus/inward/record.url?partnerID=10&rel=3.0.0&view=basic&eid=2-s2.0-85172878670&md5=255a80d1feea84b2ee6769bd9c14f9e","scopusEid":"2-s2.0-85172878670","thirdParty":false,"volume":"Volume 15","abstract":{}},{"articleTitle":"Geoenvironmental investigation of Sahure’s pyramid, Abusir archeological site, Giza, Egypt","authors":"Fahmy A., Molina-Piernas E., Helmi F.M.","doi":"10.1186/s40494-022-00699-1","externalArticle":true,"openAccess":0,"publicationDate":"December 2022","publicationTitle":"Heritage Science","publicationYear":"2022","scopusArticleUrl":"http://www.scopus.com/scopus/inward/record.url?partnerID=10&rel=3.0.0&view=basic&eid=2-s2.0-85130249605&md5=b4b6c5ecf621d4ae88e8ea69731229b1","scopusEid":"2-s2.0-85130249605","thirdParty":false,"volume":"Volume 10","abstract":{}},{"articleTitle":"MasterSu: The Sustainable Development of Su Embroidery Based on Digital Technology","authors":"Zhang L., Li M., Wang Y.","doi":"10.3390/su14127094","externalArticle":true,"openAccess":0,"publicationDate":"June-2 2022","publicationTitle":"Sustainability (Switzerland)","publicationYear":"2022","scopusArticleUrl":"http://www.scopus.com/scopus/inward/record.url?partnerID=10&rel=3.0.0&view=basic&eid=2-s2.0-85132728871&md5=53ebc126c3ea1ae45fc277fbf7592c4a","scopusEid":"2-s2.0-85132728871","thirdParty":false,"volume":"Volume 14","abstract":{}}]},"clientConfig":{"adobeSuite":"elsevier-sd-prod","arsUrl":"https://ars.els-cdn.com","assetRoute":"https://sdfestaticassets-eu-west-1.sciencedirectassets.com/prod/ce56d3430d01b90ea9bc07462811291c220fca6c","recommendationsFeedback":{"enabled":true,"url":"https://feedback.recs.d.elsevier.com/raw/events","timeout":60000},"googleMapsApiKey":"AIzaSyCBYU6I6lrbEU6wQXUEIte3NwGtm3jwHQc","mediaBaseUrl":"https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/","xocsMediaPlayerBaseUrl":"https://sdfestaticassets-eu-west-1.sciencedirectassets.com/shared-assets/147/js/ableplayer","strictMode":false,"seamlessAccess":{"enableSeamlessAccess":true,"scriptUrl":"https://unpkg.com/@theidentityselector/thiss-ds@1.0.13/dist/thiss-ds.js","persistenceUrl":"https://service.seamlessaccess.org/ps/","persistenceContext":"seamlessaccess.org","scienceDirectUrl":"https://www.sciencedirect.com","shibAuthUrl":"https://auth.elsevier.com/ShibAuth/institutionLogin"},"reaxys":{"apiUrl":"https://reaxys-sdlc.reaxys.com","origin":"sciencedirect","queryBuilderHostPath":"https://www.reaxys.com/reaxys/secured/hopinto.do","url":"https://www.reaxys.com"},"oneTrustCookie":{"enabled":true},"ssrn":{"url":"https://papers.ssrn.com","path":"/sol3/papers.cfm"},"aiNavigation":{"enabled":true,"version":"V1"}},"combinedContentItems":{"content":[{"#name":"keywords","$$":[{"#name":"keywords","$":{"xmlns:ce":true,"xmlns:aep":true,"xmlns:xoe":true,"xmlns:mml":true,"xmlns:xs":true,"xmlns:xlink":true,"xmlns:xocs":true,"xmlns:tb":true,"xmlns:xsi":true,"xmlns:cals":true,"xmlns:sb":true,"xmlns:sa":true,"xmlns:ja":true,"xmlns":true,"class":"keyword","id":"keys0001","view":"all"},"$$":[{"#name":"section-title","$":{"id":"cesectitle0004"},"_":"Keywords"},{"#name":"keyword","$":{"id":"key0001"},"$$":[{"#name":"text","$":{"id":"cetext0002"},"_":"Hittite"}]},{"#name":"keyword","$":{"id":"key0002"},"$$":[{"#name":"text","$":{"id":"cetext0003"},"_":"Hattusa"}]},{"#name":"keyword","$":{"id":"key0003"},"$$":[{"#name":"text","$":{"id":"cetext0004"},"_":"Stone deterioration"}]},{"#name":"keyword","$":{"id":"key0004"},"$$":[{"#name":"text","$":{"id":"cetext0005"},"_":"Deterioration map"}]},{"#name":"keyword","$":{"id":"key0005"},"$$":[{"#name":"text","$":{"id":"cetext0006"},"_":"Mask R-CNN"}]}]}]}],"floats":[],"footnotes":[],"attachments":[]},"crossMark":{"isOpen":false},"domainConfig":{"assetRoute":"https://sdfestaticassets-eu-west-1.sciencedirectassets.com/prod/ce56d3430d01b90ea9bc07462811291c220fca6c","cdnAssetsHost":"https://sdfestaticassets-eu-west-1.sciencedirectassets.com"},"downloadIssue":{"openOnPageLoad":false,"isOpen":false,"downloadCapOpen":false,"articles":[],"selected":[]},"enrichedContent":{"tableOfContents":false,"researchData":{"hasResearchData":false,"dataProfile":{},"openData":{},"mendeleyData":{},"databaseLinking":{}},"geospatialData":{"attachments":[]},"interactiveCaseInsights":{},"virtualMicroscope":{}},"entitledRecommendations":{"openOnPageLoad":false,"isOpen":false,"articles":[],"selected":[],"currentPage":1,"totalPages":1},"exam":{},"helpText":{"keyDates":{"html":"<div class=\"key-dates-help\"><h3 class=\"u-margin-s-bottom u-h4\">Publication milestones</h3><p class=\"u-margin-m-bottom\">The dates displayed for an article provide information on when various publication milestones were reached at the journal that has published the article. Where applicable, activities on preceding journals at which the article was previously under consideration are not shown (for instance submission, revisions, rejection).</p><p class=\"u-margin-xs-bottom\">The publication milestones include:</p><ul class=\"key-dates-help-list u-margin-m-bottom u-padding-s-left\"><li><span class=\"u-text-italic\">Received</span>: The date the article was originally submitted to the journal.</li><li><span class=\"u-text-italic\">Revised</span>: The date the most recent revision of the article was submitted to the journal. Dates corresponding to intermediate revisions are not shown.</li><li><span class=\"u-text-italic\">Accepted</span>: The date the article was accepted for publication in the journal.</li><li><span class=\"u-text-italic\">Available online</span>: The date a version of the article was made available online in the journal.</li><li><span class=\"u-text-italic\">Version of Record</span>: The date the finalized version of the article was made available in the journal.</li></ul><p>More information on publishing policies can be found on the <a class=\"anchor anchor-secondary u-display-inline anchor-underline\" href=\"https://www.elsevier.com/about/policies-and-standards/publishing-ethics\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"anchor-text-container\"><span class=\"anchor-text\">Publishing Ethics Policies</span></span></a> page. View our <a class=\"anchor anchor-secondary u-display-inline anchor-underline\" href=\"https://www.elsevier.com/researcher/author/submit-your-paper\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"anchor-text-container\"><span class=\"anchor-text\">Publishing with Elsevier: step-by-step</span></span></a> page to learn more about the publishing process. For any questions on your own submission or other questions related to publishing an article, <a class=\"anchor anchor-secondary u-display-inline anchor-underline\" href=\"https://service.elsevier.com/app/phone/supporthub/publishing\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"anchor-text-container\"><span class=\"anchor-text\">contact our Researcher support team.</span></span></a></p></div>","title":"What do these dates mean?"}},"glossary":{},"issueNavigation":{"previous":{},"next":{}},"linkingHubLinks":{},"metrics":{"metricGroup":{"citations":[],"captures":[],"mentions":[],"socialMedia":[]},"isLoading":false,"error":false,"hasMetrics":false},"preview":{"content":[{"#name":"introduction","$$":[{"#name":"section","$":{"xmlns:ce":true,"xmlns:mml":true,"xmlns:xs":true,"xmlns:xlink":true,"xmlns:xocs":true,"xmlns:tb":true,"xmlns:xsi":true,"xmlns:cals":true,"xmlns:sb":true,"xmlns:sa":true,"xmlns:ja":true,"xmlns":true,"id":"sec0001","role":"introduction","view":"all"},"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"1"},{"#name":"section-title","$":{"id":"cesectitle0005"},"_":"Introduction"},{"#name":"para","$":{"id":"para0005","view":"all"},"_":"Archeological sites are heritage areas that enlighten the collective memories of past societies, such as their habitats, esthetic concerns, and beliefs. On movable and immovable cultural heritage, the conservation of stone, which is the most used material in these areas helping reconstruct history, is threatened by various deterioration processes due to natural or anthropogenic causes. When movable cultural heritage (such as statues, tablets and jewelry, cookware) is brought to the surface by archeological excavations, the process of identifying problems in works and planning restoration interventions can be quite fast. In immovable cultural heritage (palaces, churches, rock reliefs, etc.), periodic inspections should be performed in addition to this process. If the necessary inspection and measures are not taken in both types of heritage, a small-scale (e.g. flaking, spalling, cracking) deterioration type can turn into a larger-scale anomaly (such as contour scaling, differential erosion, missing part) over time, and this may cause the loss of an archeological trace. Therefore, the identification of deterioration in the preservation of heritage in archeological sites constitutes one of the most important stages of restoration studies. However, the size of these areas makes it difficult to examine the types of deterioration in each work in periodic time frames. Moreover, the current inspections performed by experts by on-site visual analysis can also lead to human-induced errors. Developing computer-aided methods may be an important option in solving this situation. To reduce the workforce and margin of error in archeological sites and achieve long-term comparability on-site as well as among different sites, computer-aided methods such as remote sensing [1], relief extraction [2], automatic drawing [3], and photogrammetry [4] were first used. Afterward, by supporting devices such as terrestrial laser scanning and unmanned aerial vehicles with computer software, digital reconstruction [5], surface erosion examination [6], and pixel-based archeological feature detection [7] studies have been conducted. However, although all these methods have significant advantages in heritage sites, they have inadequacies in identifying deterioration. The most important one of these inadequacies is that deterioration groups are different from each other in shape, texture, and color. Therefore, expert opinion is needed to interpret the data during the identification. Other disadvantages of these methods are that they are expensive and their data processing time is long. To overcome these problems, the deep learning (DL) method, which can learn human skills with a large number of data entries, can be an effective tool. It is possible to create object recognition algorithms with high accuracy and precision with DL, an artificial intelligence method. With this feature, some studies [8], [9], [10] have been recently conducted in archeological sites using DL, which has become widespread in different disciplines. In their study, Bewes et al. [8] predicted sex from skeletal remains using DL and obtained 95% accuracy values. Verschoof-van der Vaart and Lambers [9] identified prehistoric barrows and Celtic fields using the Faster R-CNN algorithm. Cintas et al. [10] used the pottery profile classification method and obtained a mean accuracy score of 0.9013. Apart from these studies, there are also damage assessment studies in the limited components of historic monuments (brick walls, building stones, and tiles) [11], [12], [13], [14]. In their study, Wang et al. [11] tried to determine spalling and efflorescence type deterioration in a historic brick wall in China using the Faster R-CNN algorithm. Hatır et al. [12] classified eight different deterioration types observed in building stones in the Konya region and fresh rock using the DL method in their study. Wang et al. [13] determined the damage observed in historic glazed tiles using the Mask R-CNN algorithm. In their study, Hatır and İnce [14] demonstrated that the lithologies of the building stones used on the southern facade of the Matbah-ı Şerif monument could be determined by the DL method. These studies in the literature show that artificial intelligence algorithms yield extremely important results in the fields of anthropology, archeology, and structural engineering."},{"#name":"para","$":{"id":"para0006","view":"all"},"_":"In previous studies, applications in which a limited amount of deterioration (spalling, efflorescence, and damage) was detected in building materials were made. However, there are no studies on reliefs where detection is more difficult. In addition, there are deficiencies in the literature for determining the types of deterioration in archeological sites via computer vision. To eliminate these deficiencies, automatic deterioration detection and mapping using the Mask R-CNN method in reliefs, an integral part of cultural heritage, is targeted."}]}]},{"#name":"section","$$":[{"#name":"section","$":{"xmlns:ce":true,"xmlns:mml":true,"xmlns:xs":true,"xmlns:xlink":true,"xmlns:xocs":true,"xmlns:tb":true,"xmlns:xsi":true,"xmlns:cals":true,"xmlns:sb":true,"xmlns:sa":true,"xmlns:ja":true,"xmlns":true,"id":"sec0002","view":"all"},"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"2"},{"#name":"section-title","$":{"id":"cesectitle0006"},"_":"Research aim"},{"#name":"para","$":{"id":"para0007","view":"all"},"_":"This study aims to develop a DL algorithm that can generate the automatic mapping of the types of deterioration in archeological heritage sites with fast and accurate detection. To this end, seven different types of deterioration (missing part, contour scaling, crack, microkarst, impact damage, biological colonization, higher plant), which are generally observed in the open-air sanctuary of Yazılıkaya located nearby the Hattusa (Çorum, Turkey) archeological site and inscribed on the UNESCO"}]}]},{"#name":"section","$$":[{"#name":"section","$":{"xmlns:ce":true,"xmlns:mml":true,"xmlns:xs":true,"xmlns:xlink":true,"xmlns:xocs":true,"xmlns:tb":true,"xmlns:xsi":true,"xmlns:cals":true,"xmlns:sb":true,"xmlns:sa":true,"xmlns:ja":true,"xmlns":true,"id":"sec0003","view":"all"},"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"3"},{"#name":"section-title","$":{"id":"cesectitle0007"},"_":"Archeological frameworks of the study"},{"#name":"para","$":{"id":"para0008","view":"all"},"_":"The archeological site of Boğazköy has been identified as the ancient city of Hattusa, the capital city of the Hittite Empire, one of the major political powers during the 2nd millennium BC in the Near East by excavations and research ongoing since 1906 [15]. The unearthed monumental structures within the city reveal major insights into the art and history of the first state in Anatolia. The open-air rock sanctuary of Yazılıkaya is located approximately 2 km northeast of the city (Fig. 1)."}]}]},{"#name":"section","$$":[{"#name":"section","$":{"xmlns:ce":true,"xmlns:mml":true,"xmlns:xs":true,"xmlns:xlink":true,"xmlns:xocs":true,"xmlns:tb":true,"xmlns:xsi":true,"xmlns:cals":true,"xmlns:sb":true,"xmlns:sa":true,"xmlns:ja":true,"xmlns":true,"id":"sec0006","role":"materials-methods","view":"all"},"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"4"},{"#name":"section-title","$":{"id":"cesectitle0010"},"_":"Material and method"},{"#name":"para","$":{"id":"para0024","view":"all"},"_":"Artificial intelligence algorithms, which basically try to imitate human skills, have recently shown significant developments. Deep learning, one of these algorithms, is a method inspired by the process that a person sees and processes in his brain. The study performed by LeCun et al. [31] and based on the principle of moving pixels on the image via Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) constitutes the first step of developments in this method. However, this algorithm can perform only rough"}]}]},{"#name":"section","$$":[{"#name":"section","$":{"xmlns:ce":true,"xmlns:mml":true,"xmlns:xs":true,"xmlns:xlink":true,"xmlns:xocs":true,"xmlns:tb":true,"xmlns:xsi":true,"xmlns:cals":true,"xmlns:sb":true,"xmlns:sa":true,"xmlns:ja":true,"xmlns":true,"id":"sec0008","role":"results","view":"all"},"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"5"},{"#name":"section-title","$":{"id":"cesectitle0012"},"_":"Experimental results"},{"#name":"para","$":{"id":"para0029","view":"all"},"_":"The total loss graphs showing how far the estimates of the models developed in the study at the end of the training phase are from the ground truth are presented in Fig. 6. The loss value approaching 0 increases the model's estimation reliability, and it is observed to approach 0.1 for 26,000 iterations in the model developed for the deposit (Fig. 6a). In model 2, this value approaches 0.23 (Fig. 6b). The training success of the models was determined by average precision (AP) and mean average"}]}]},{"#name":"section","$$":[{"#name":"section","$":{"xmlns:ce":true,"xmlns:mml":true,"xmlns:xs":true,"xmlns:xlink":true,"xmlns:xocs":true,"xmlns:tb":true,"xmlns:xsi":true,"xmlns:cals":true,"xmlns:sb":true,"xmlns:sa":true,"xmlns:ja":true,"xmlns":true,"id":"sec0009","role":"conclusion","view":"all"},"$$":[{"#name":"label","_":"6"},{"#name":"section-title","$":{"id":"cesectitle0013"},"_":"Conclusion"},{"#name":"para","$":{"id":"para0036","view":"all"},"_":"The periodic control of deterioration in archeological heritage sites is extremely important for preserving artifacts and transferring them to future generations. However, due to the size of archeological sites and the necessary vision-based controls, the duration of examination increases, which may cause the occurrence of possible human-induced errors. In this study, two different computer vision models were developed using the deep learning algorithm to prevent possible errors and"}]}]},{"#name":"section","$$":[{"#name":"acknowledgment","$":{"xmlns:ce":true,"xmlns:mml":true,"xmlns:xs":true,"xmlns:xlink":true,"xmlns:xocs":true,"xmlns:tb":true,"xmlns:xsi":true,"xmlns:cals":true,"xmlns:sb":true,"xmlns:sa":true,"xmlns:ja":true,"xmlns":true,"id":"ack0001","view":"all"},"$$":[{"#name":"section-title","$":{"id":"cesectitle0014"},"_":"Acknowledgments"},{"#name":"para","$":{"id":"para0041","view":"all"},"_":"We would like to thank the Ministry of Culture and Tourism, Çorum Provincial Directorate of Culture and Tourism, Çorum Museum Directorate, and the German Archeology Institute for granting the necessary permissions to work in the field. Moreover, the authors are thankful to the reviewers for their constructive comments."}]}]}],"floats":[],"footnotes":[],"attachments":[]},"rawtext":"","recommendations":{"articles":[{"pii":"S1296207422000012","doi":"10.1016/j.culher.2022.01.001","journalTitle":"Jo