2 51.86 2 64s9.86 22 22 22c6.38 0 12.12-2.76 16.14-7.12l28.64 15.38c-.5 1.84-.78 3.76-.78 5.74 0 12.14 9.86 22 22 22s22-9.86 22-22-9.86-22-22-22z"></path></svg><span class="button-link-text-container"><span class="button-link-text">Share</span></span></button></div></div></div><div class="ExportCitation u-display-inline-block" id="export-citation"><div class="popover export-citation-popover" id="export-citation-popover"><div id="popover-trigger-export-citation-popover"><button class="button-link button-link-secondary button-link-icon-left button-link-has-colored-icon" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-haspopup="true"><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 104 128" height="20" class="icon icon-cited-by-66"><path d="M2 58.78V106h44V64H12v-5.22C12 40.28 29.08 32 46 32V22C20.1 22 2 37.12 2 58.78zM102 32V22c-25.9 0-44 15.12-44 36.78V106h44V64H68v-5.22C68 40.28 85.08 32 102 32z"></path></svg><span class="button-link-text-container"><span class="button-link-text">Cite</span></span></button></div></div></div></div></div><div class="ArticleIdentifierLinks u-margin-xs-bottom text-xs" id="article-identifier-links"><a class="anchor doi anchor-primary" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.culher.2021.07.004" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label="Persistent link using digital object identifier" title="Persistent link using digital object identifier"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.culher.2021.07.004</span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 8 8" height="20" aria-label="Opens in new window" class="icon icon-arrow-up-right-tiny arrow-external-link"><path d="M1.12949 2.1072V1H7V6.85795H5.89111V2.90281L0.784057 8L0 7.21635L5.11902 2.1072H1.12949Z"></path></svg></span></a><a class="anchor rights-and-content anchor-primary" href="https://s100.copyright.com/AppDispatchServlet?publisherName=ELS&amp;contentID=S1296207421001096&amp;orderBeanReset=true" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Get rights and content</span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 8 8" height="20" aria-label="Opens in new window" class="icon icon-arrow-up-right-tiny arrow-external-link"><path d="M1.12949 2.1072V1H7V6.85795H5.89111V2.90281L0.784057 8L0 7.21635L5.11902 2.1072H1.12949Z"></path></svg></span></a></div><section class="ReferencedArticles"></section><section class="ReferencedArticles"></section><div id="preview-section-abstract"><div class="PageDivider"></div><div class="Abstracts u-font-serif" id="abstracts"><div class="abstract author-highlights" id="abs0001"><h2 class="section-title u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Highlights</h2><div id="abss0001"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="spara015"><ul class="list"><li class="react-xocs-list-item"><span class="list-label">•</span><span class="list-content"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="para0002">Deterioration development in archeological sites.</div></span></li><li class="react-xocs-list-item"><span class="list-label">•</span><span class="list-content"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="para0003">Determination of stone deterioration via artificial intelligence.</div></span></li><li class="react-xocs-list-item"><span class="list-label">•</span><span class="list-content"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="para0004">Deterioration map with Mask R-CNN.</div></span></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="abstract author" id="abs0002"><h2 class="section-title u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Abstract</h2><div id="abss0002"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="spara016">The detection of deterioration in archeological heritage sites is a very time-consuming task that requires expertise. Furthermore, vision-based approaches can cause errors, considering the complex types of deterioration that develop in different scales and forms in monuments. This problem can be solved effectively owing to computer vision algorithms, commonly used in different areas nowadays. This study aims to develop a model that automatically detects and maps deteriorations (biological colonization, contour scaling, crack, higher plant, impact damage, microkarst, missing part) and restoration interventions using the Mask R-CNN algorithm, which has recently come to the fore with its feature of recognizing small and large-sized objects. To this end, a total of 2460 images of Yazılıkaya monuments in the Hattusa archeological site, which is on the UNESCO heritage list, were gathered. In the training phase of the proposed method, it was trained in model 1 to distinguish deposit deterioration commonly observed on the surface of monuments from other anomalies. Other anomalies trained were model 2. In this phase of the models, the average precision values with high accuracy rates ranging from 89.624% to 100% were obtained for the deterioration classes. The developed algorithms were tested on 4 different rock reliefs in Yazılıkaya, which were not used in the training phase. In addition, an image of the Eflatunpınar water monument, which is on the UNESCO tentative list, was used to test the model&#x27;s universality. According to the test results, it was determined that the models could be successfully applied to obtain maps of deterioration and restoration interventions in monuments in different regions.</div></div></div><div class="abstract graphical" id="abs0003"><h2 class="section-title u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Graphical abstract</h2><div id="abss0003"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="spara017"><span class="display"><figure class="figure text-xs" id="fig0012"><span><img src="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1296207421001096-ga1.jpg" height="245" alt="Image, graphical abstract"/><ol class="u-margin-s-bottom"><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans anchor-primary" href="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1296207421001096-ga1_lrg.jpg" target="_blank" download="" title="Download high-res image (285KB)"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Download: <span class="download-link-title">Download high-res image (285KB)</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans anchor-primary" href="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1296207421001096-ga1.jpg" target="_blank" download="" title="Download full-size image"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Download: <span class="download-link-title">Download full-size image</span></span></span></a></li></ol></span></figure></span></div></div></div></div></div><div id="preview-section-introduction"><div class="PageDivider"></div><div class="Introduction u-font-serif u-margin-l-ver"><h2 class="u-h4 u-margin-s-bottom">Introduction</h2><section id="sec0001"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="para0005">Archeological sites are heritage areas that enlighten the collective memories of past societies, such as their habitats, esthetic concerns, and beliefs. On movable and immovable cultural heritage, the conservation of stone, which is the most used material in these areas helping reconstruct history, is threatened by various deterioration processes due to natural or anthropogenic causes. When movable cultural heritage (such as statues, tablets and jewelry, cookware) is brought to the surface by archeological excavations, the process of identifying problems in works and planning restoration interventions can be quite fast. In immovable cultural heritage (palaces, churches, rock reliefs, etc.), periodic inspections should be performed in addition to this process. If the necessary inspection and measures are not taken in both types of heritage, a small-scale (e.g. flaking, spalling, cracking) deterioration type can turn into a larger-scale anomaly (such as contour scaling, differential erosion, missing part) over time, and this may cause the loss of an archeological trace. Therefore, the identification of deterioration in the preservation of heritage in archeological sites constitutes one of the most important stages of restoration studies. However, the size of these areas makes it difficult to examine the types of deterioration in each work in periodic time frames. Moreover, the current inspections performed by experts by on-site visual analysis can also lead to human-induced errors. Developing computer-aided methods may be an important option in solving this situation. To reduce the workforce and margin of error in archeological sites and achieve long-term comparability on-site as well as among different sites, computer-aided methods such as remote sensing [1], relief extraction [2], automatic drawing [3], and photogrammetry [4] were first used. Afterward, by supporting devices such as terrestrial laser scanning and unmanned aerial vehicles with computer software, digital reconstruction [5], surface erosion examination [6], and pixel-based archeological feature detection [7] studies have been conducted. However, although all these methods have significant advantages in heritage sites, they have inadequacies in identifying deterioration. The most important one of these inadequacies is that deterioration groups are different from each other in shape, texture, and color. Therefore, expert opinion is needed to interpret the data during the identification. Other disadvantages of these methods are that they are expensive and their data processing time is long. To overcome these problems, the deep learning (DL) method, which can learn human skills with a large number of data entries, can be an effective tool. It is possible to create object recognition algorithms with high accuracy and precision with DL, an artificial intelligence method. With this feature, some studies [8], [9], [10] have been recently conducted in archeological sites using DL, which has become widespread in different disciplines. In their study, Bewes et al. [8] predicted sex from skeletal remains using DL and obtained 95% accuracy values. Verschoof-van der Vaart and Lambers [9] identified prehistoric barrows and Celtic fields using the Faster R-CNN algorithm. Cintas et al. [10] used the pottery profile classification method and obtained a mean accuracy score of 0.9013. Apart from these studies, there are also damage assessment studies in the limited components of historic monuments (brick walls, building stones, and tiles) [11], [12], [13], [14]. In their study, Wang et al. [11] tried to determine spalling and efflorescence type deterioration in a historic brick wall in China using the Faster R-CNN algorithm. Hatır et al. [12] classified eight different deterioration types observed in building stones in the Konya region and fresh rock using the DL method in their study. Wang et al. [13] determined the damage observed in historic glazed tiles using the Mask R-CNN algorithm. In their study, Hatır and İnce [14] demonstrated that the lithologies of the building stones used on the southern facade of the Matbah-ı Şerif monument could be determined by the DL method. These studies in the literature show that artificial intelligence algorithms yield extremely important results in the fields of anthropology, archeology, and structural engineering.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="para0006">In previous studies, applications in which a limited amount of deterioration (spalling, efflorescence, and damage) was detected in building materials were made. However, there are no studies on reliefs where detection is more difficult. In addition, there are deficiencies in the literature for determining the types of deterioration in archeological sites via computer vision. To eliminate these deficiencies, automatic deterioration detection and mapping using the Mask R-CNN method in reliefs, an integral part of cultural heritage, is targeted.</div></section></div></div><div id="preview-section-snippets"><div class="PageDivider"></div><div class="Snippets u-font-serif"><h2 class="u-h4 u-margin-l-ver">Section snippets</h2><section><section id="sec0002"><h2 class="section-title u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Research aim</h2><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="para0007">This study aims to develop a DL algorithm that can generate the automatic mapping of the types of deterioration in archeological heritage sites with fast and accurate detection. To this end, seven different types of deterioration (missing part, contour scaling, crack, microkarst, impact damage, biological colonization, higher plant), which are generally observed in the open-air sanctuary of Yazılıkaya located nearby the Hattusa (Çorum, Turkey) archeological site and inscribed on the UNESCO</div></section></section><section><section id="sec0003"><h2 class="section-title u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Archeological frameworks of the study</h2><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="para0008">The archeological site of Boğazköy has been identified as the ancient city of Hattusa, the capital city of the Hittite Empire, one of the major political powers during the 2nd millennium BC in the Near East by excavations and research ongoing since 1906 [15]. The unearthed monumental structures within the city reveal major insights into the art and history of the first state in Anatolia. The open-air rock sanctuary of Yazılıkaya is located approximately 2 km northeast of the city (Fig. 1).</div></section></section><section><section id="sec0006"><h2 class="section-title u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Material and method</h2><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="para0024">Artificial intelligence algorithms, which basically try to imitate human skills, have recently shown significant developments. Deep learning, one of these algorithms, is a method inspired by the process that a person sees and processes in his brain. The study performed by LeCun et al. [31] and based on the principle of moving pixels on the image via Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) constitutes the first step of developments in this method. However, this algorithm can perform only rough</div></section></section><section><section id="sec0008"><h2 class="section-title u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Experimental results</h2><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="para0029">The total loss graphs showing how far the estimates of the models developed in the study at the end of the training phase are from the ground truth are presented in Fig. 6. The loss value approaching 0 increases the model&#x27;s estimation reliability, and it is observed to approach 0.1 for 26,000 iterations in the model developed for the deposit (Fig. 6a). In model 2, this value approaches 0.23 (Fig. 6b). The training success of the models was determined by average precision (AP) and mean average</div></section></section><section><section id="sec0009"><h2 class="section-title u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Conclusion</h2><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="para0036">The periodic control of deterioration in archeological heritage sites is extremely important for preserving artifacts and transferring them to future generations. However, due to the size of archeological sites and the necessary vision-based controls, the duration of examination increases, which may cause the occurrence of possible human-induced errors. In this study, two different computer vision models were developed using the deep learning algorithm to prevent possible errors and</div></section></section><section><section id="ack0001"><h2 id="cesectitle0014" class="u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Acknowledgments</h2><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="para0041">We would like to thank the Ministry of Culture and Tourism, Çorum Provincial Directorate of Culture and Tourism, Çorum Museum Directorate, and the German Archeology Institute for granting the necessary permissions to work in the field. Moreover, the authors are thankful to the reviewers for their constructive comments.</div></section></section></div></div><div class="related-content-links u-display-none-from-md"><button class="button-link button-link-primary button-link-small" type="button"><span class="button-link-text-container"><span class="button-link-text">Recommended articles</span></span></button></div><div class="Tail"></div><div id="preview-section-references"><div class="paginatedReferences u-font-serif"><div class="PageDivider"></div><header><h2 class="u-h4 u-margin-l-ver"><span>References</span><span> (35)</span></h2></header><ul><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>V. </span>De Laet</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3><a class="anchor title anchor-primary" href="/science/article/pii/S0305440306001774"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Methods for the extraction of archaeological features from very high-resolution Ikonos-2 remote sensing imagery, Hisar (southwest Turkey)</span></span></a></h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">J. Archaeol. Sci.</h3></div><div class="series">(2007)</div></span></li><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>A. </span>Gilboa</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3><a class="anchor title anchor-primary" href="/science/article/pii/S0305440312004207"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Computer-based, automatic recording and illustration of complex archaeological artifacts</span></span></a></h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">J. Archaeol. Sci.</h3></div><div class="series">(2013)</div></span></li><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>J. </span>Zhang</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3><a class="anchor title anchor-primary" href="/science/article/pii/S1296207418302668"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Computer simulation of archaeological drawings</span></span></a></h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">J. Cult. Herit.</h3></div><div class="series">(2019)</div></span></li><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>F.I. </span>Apollonio</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3><a class="anchor title anchor-primary" href="/science/article/pii/S030544031100481X"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">3D reality-based artefact models for the management of archaeological sites using 3D Gis: a framework starting from the case study of the Pompeii Archaeological area</span></span></a></h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">J. Archaeol. Sci.</h3></div><div class="series">(2012)</div></span></li><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>M. </span>Kincey</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3><a class="anchor title anchor-primary" href="/science/article/pii/S2352409X17300500"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Quantifying erosion of ‘at risk’ archaeological sites using repeat terrestrial laser scanning</span></span></a></h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">J. Archaeol. Sci. Rep.</h3></div><div class="series">(2017)</div></span></li><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>C. </span>Sevara</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3><a class="anchor title anchor-primary" href="/science/article/pii/S2352409X15302248"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Pixel versus object-A comparison of strategies for the semi-automated mapping of archaeological features using airborne laser scanning data</span></span></a></h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">J. Archaeol. Sci. Rep.</h3></div><div class="series">(2016)</div></span></li><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>J. </span>Bewes</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3><a class="anchor title anchor-primary" href="/science/article/pii/S1752928X18304219"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Artificial intelligence for sex determination of skeletal remains: application of a deep learning artificial neural network to human skulls</span></span></a></h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">J. Forensic Leg. Med.</h3></div><div class="series">(2019)</div></span></li><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>C. </span>Cintas</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3><a class="anchor title anchor-primary" href="/science/article/pii/S1296207418307775"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Automatic feature extraction and classification of Iberian ceramics based on deep convolutional networks</span></span></a></h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">J. Cult. Herit.</h3></div><div class="series">(2020)</div></span></li><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>N. </span>Wang</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3><a class="anchor title anchor-primary" href="/science/article/pii/S0926580518307568"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Automatic damage detection of historic masonry buildings based on mobile deep learning</span></span></a></h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">Autom. Construct.</h3></div><div class="series">(2019)</div></span></li><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>M.E. </span>Hatır</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3><a class="anchor title anchor-primary" href="/science/article/pii/S1296207420300947"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Deep learning-based weathering type recognition in historical stone monuments</span></span></a></h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">J. Cult. Herit.</h3></div><div class="series">(2020)</div></span></li></ul><div class="u-display-none"><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>B. </span>Fitzner</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3><a class="anchor title anchor-primary" href="/science/article/pii/S0360132303000866"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Weathering damage on pharaonic sandstone monuments in luxor-Egypt</span></span></a></h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">build Environ. Times</h3></div><div class="series">(2003)</div></span></li><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>M. </span>Korkanç</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3><a class="anchor title anchor-primary" href="/science/article/pii/S0950061815001075"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Impact of the surface roughness of stones used in historical buildings on biodeterioration</span></span></a></h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">Constr. Build. Mater.</h3></div><div class="series">(2015)</div></span></li><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>A. </span>Bozdağ</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3 class="title">An assessment of deterioration in cultural heritage: the unique case of Eflatunpınar Hittite Water Monument in Konya, Turkey</h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">Bull. Eng. Geol. Environ.</h3></div><div class="series">(2020)</div></span></li><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>W.B. </span>Verschoof-van der Vaart</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3 class="title">Learning to Look at LiDAR: the use of R-CNN in the automated detection of archaeological objects in LiDAR data from the Netherlands</h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">J. Comput. Appl. Archaeol.</h3></div><div class="series">(2019)</div></span></li><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>N. </span>Wang</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3 class="title">Autonomous damage segmentation and measurement of glazed tiles in historic buildings via deep learning</h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">Comput-Aided Civ. Inf.</h3></div><div class="series">(2020)</div></span></li><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="u-font-sans"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>M.E. </span>Hatır</span><em> et al.</em></span><h3 class="title">Lithology mapping of stone heritage via state-of-the-art computer vision</h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series"><h3 class="title">J. Build. Eng</h3></div><div class="series">(2021)</div></span></li><li class="bib-reference u-margin-s-bottom"><span class="author u-font-sans"><span>A. </span>Schachner</span><h3 class="title">Hattuscha. Auf den Spuren der Sagenhaften Hauptstadt der Hethiter</h3><span class="host u-clr-grey6 u-font-sans"><div class="series">(2011)</div></span></li></div><button class="button-alternative button-alternative-secondary u-font-sans u-margin-l-bottom large-alternative button-alternative-icon-left" type="button" id="show-more-refs-btn"><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 92 128" height="20" class="icon icon-navigate-down"><path d="M1 51l7-7 38 38 38-38 7 7-45 45z"></path></svg><span class="button-alternative-text-container"><span class="button-alternative-text">View more references</span></span></button></div></div><div id="preview-section-cited-by"><section aria-label="Cited by" class="ListArticles preview"><div class="PageDivider"></div><header id="citing-articles-header"><h2 class="u-h4 u-margin-l-ver u-font-serif">Cited by (33)</h2></header><div aria-describedby="citing-articles-header"><div class="citing-articles u-margin-l-bottom"><ul><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class="u-font-serif" id="citing-articles-article-0-title"><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="/science/article/pii/S1296207424000050"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Artificial intelligence-assisted visual inspection for cultural heritage: State-of-the-art review</span></span></a></h3><div>2024, Journal of Cultural Heritage</div></div><div class="buttons"><button class="button-link button-link-primary button-link-icon-right" type="button" data-aa-button="sd:product:journal:article:location=citing-articles:type=view-details" aria-describedby="citing-articles-article-0-title" aria-controls="citing-articles-article-0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="button-link-text-container"><span class="button-link-text">Show abstract</span></span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 92 128" height="20" class="icon icon-navigate-down"><path d="M1 51l7-7 38 38 38-38 7 7-45 45z"></path></svg></button></div><div class="u-display-none" aria-hidden="true"><div class="abstract u-margin-xs-top u-margin-m-bottom u-font-serif" id="reference-abstract"><div class="u-margin-ver-m"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="sp0002">Applying computer science techniques such as artificial intelligence (AI), deep learning (DL), and computer vision (CV) on digital image data can help monitor and preserve cultural heritage (CH) sites. Defects such as weathering, removal of mortar, joint damage, discoloration, erosion, surface cracks, vegetation, seepage, and vandalism and their propagation with time adversely affect the structural health of CH sites. Several studies have reported damage detection in concrete and bridge structures using AI techniques. However, few studies have quantified defects in CH structures using the AI paradigm, and limited case studies exist for their applications. Hence, the application of AI-assisted visual inspections for CH sites needs to be explored. AI-assisted digital inspections assist inspection professionals and increase confidence levels in the damage assessment of CH buildings. This review summarizes the damage assessment techniques using image processing techniques, focusing mainly on DL techniques applied for CH conservation. Several case study applications of CH buildings are presented where AI can assist in traditional visual inspections.</div></div></div></div></li><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class="u-font-serif" id="citing-articles-article-1-title"><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="/science/article/pii/S0926580523001887"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Preserving our heritage: A photogrammetry-based digital twin framework for monitoring deteriorations of historic structures</span></span></a></h3><div>2023, Automation in Construction</div><div class="CitedSection u-margin-s-top"><div class="u-margin-s-left"><div class="cite-header u-text-italic u-font-sans">Citation Excerpt :</div><p class="u-font-serif text-xs">Pathak et al. [15] trained a convolution neural network to classify spalling and cracks from an image dataset of a historic temple in Thailand. Hatır al [16] leveraged R-CNN to detect multiple types of stone deteriorations and further mapped them back to orthophotos. For a comprehensive review of recent advances in machine learning-based methods in SHM of historic structures, the readers are referred to [17].</p></div></div></div><div class="buttons"><button class="button-link button-link-primary button-link-icon-right" type="button" data-aa-button="sd:product:journal:article:location=citing-articles:type=view-details" aria-describedby="citing-articles-article-1-title" aria-controls="citing-articles-article-1" aria-expanded="false"><span class="button-link-text-container"><span class="button-link-text">Show abstract</span></span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 92 128" height="20" class="icon icon-navigate-down"><path d="M1 51l7-7 38 38 38-38 7 7-45 45z"></path></svg></button></div><div class="u-display-none" aria-hidden="true"><div class="abstract u-margin-xs-top u-margin-m-bottom u-font-serif" id="reference-abstract"><div class="u-margin-ver-m"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="sp0115">Preservation of historic structures is a global challenge for governments and site owners, as accumulated deteriorations could weaken the structural integrity. Recent advances in the digital twin in civil and infrastructure engineering have shown great promise in creating virtual models of a physical entity under time-varying physical states. Because of this unique feature, assessing time-varying structural deteriorations become feasible. This paper presents a novel methodological attempt of health monitoring for historic structures by integrating photogrammetry technologies and point cloud processing algorithms into a digital twin framework. To make this viable, virtual models of the physical structure at different inspection times are first created <em>via</em> photogrammetry technologies. Then, a bridge health monitoring method is established to align two virtual models together and further differentiate the changes provoked by structural deterioration. We validate the proposed methodology in a historic stone arch bridge for detecting multiple simulated structural deteriorations.</div></div></div></div></li><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class="u-font-serif" id="citing-articles-article-2-title"><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://doi.org/10.3390/buildings13123113" target="_blank"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Deep Learning-Based Automated Detection of Cracks in Historical Masonry Structures</span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 8 8" height="20" aria-label="Opens in new window" class="icon icon-arrow-up-right-tiny arrow-external-link"><path d="M1.12949 2.1072V1H7V6.85795H5.89111V2.90281L0.784057 8L0 7.21635L5.11902 2.1072H1.12949Z"></path></svg></span></a></h3><div>2023, Buildings</div></div><div class="buttons"></div><div class="u-display-none" aria-hidden="true"><div class="abstract u-margin-xs-top u-margin-m-bottom u-font-serif" id="reference-abstract"></div></div></li><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class="u-font-serif" id="citing-articles-article-3-title"><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://doi.org/10.3390/su151813464" target="_blank"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Reviewing and Integrating AEC Practices into Industry 6.0: Strategies for Smart and Sustainable Future-Built Environments</span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 8 8" height="20" aria-label="Opens in new window" class="icon icon-arrow-up-right-tiny arrow-external-link"><path d="M1.12949 2.1072V1H7V6.85795H5.89111V2.90281L0.784057 8L0 7.21635L5.11902 2.1072H1.12949Z"></path></svg></span></a></h3><div>2023, Sustainability (Switzerland)</div></div><div class="buttons"></div><div class="u-display-none" aria-hidden="true"><div class="abstract u-margin-xs-top u-margin-m-bottom u-font-serif" id="reference-abstract"></div></div></li><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class="u-font-serif" id="citing-articles-article-4-title"><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-022-00699-1" target="_blank"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Geoenvironmental investigation of Sahure’s pyramid, Abusir archeological site, Giza, Egypt</span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 8 8" height="20" aria-label="Opens in new window" class="icon icon-arrow-up-right-tiny arrow-external-link"><path d="M1.12949 2.1072V1H7V6.85795H5.89111V2.90281L0.784057 8L0 7.21635L5.11902 2.1072H1.12949Z"></path></svg></span></a></h3><div>2022, Heritage Science</div></div><div class="buttons"></div><div class="u-display-none" aria-hidden="true"><div class="abstract u-margin-xs-top u-margin-m-bottom u-font-serif" id="reference-abstract"></div></div></li><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class="u-font-serif" id="citing-articles-article-5-title"><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://doi.org/10.3390/su14127094" target="_blank"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">MasterSu: The Sustainable Development of Su Embroidery Based on Digital Technology</span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 8 8" height="20" aria-label="Opens in new window" class="icon icon-arrow-up-right-tiny arrow-external-link"><path d="M1.12949 2.1072V1H7V6.85795H5.89111V2.90281L0.784057 8L0 7.21635L5.11902 2.1072H1.12949Z"></path></svg></span></a></h3><div>2022, Sustainability (Switzerland)</div></div><div class="buttons"></div><div class="u-display-none" aria-hidden="true"><div class="abstract u-margin-xs-top u-margin-m-bottom u-font-serif" id="reference-abstract"></div></div></li></ul><a class="button-alternative button-alternative-secondary large-alternative button-alternative-icon-left" href="http://www.scopus.com/scopus/inward/citedby.url?partnerID=10&amp;rel=3.0.0&amp;eid=2-s2.0-85111321583&amp;md5=5cd57b61fda363314c38048fa69e3a" target="_blank" id="citing-articles-view-all-btn"><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 54 128" height="20" class="icon icon-navigate-right"><path d="M1 99l38-38L1 23l7-7 45 45-45 45z"></path></svg><span class="button-alternative-text-container"><span class="button-alternative-text">View all citing articles on Scopus</span></span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 8 8" height="20" aria-label="Opens in new window" class="icon icon-arrow-up-right-tiny arrow-external-link"><path d="M1.12949 2.1072V1H7V6.85795H5.89111V2.90281L0.784057 8L0 7.21635L5.11902 2.1072H1.12949Z"></path></svg></a></div></div></section></div><div class="PageDivider"></div><div class="Footnotes text-xs"><dl class="footnote"><dt class="footnote-label"></dt><dd class="footnote-detail u-padding-xs-top"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="notep0001">Declarations of interest: none</div></dd></dl></div><a class="anchor full-text-link anchor-primary" href="/science/article/pii/S1296207421001096" aria-disabled="true" tabindex="-1"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">View full text</span></span></a><div class="Copyright"><span class="copyright-line">© 2021 Elsevier Masson SAS. All rights reserved.</span></div></article><div class="u-display-block-from-md col-lg-6 col-md-8 pad-right u-padding-s-top"><aside class="RelatedContent u-clr-grey8" aria-label="Related content"><section class="RelatedContentPanel u-margin-s-bottom"><header id="recommended-articles-header" class="related-content-panel-header u-margin-s-bottom"><button class="button-link button-link-secondary related-content-panel-toggle is-up button-link-icon-right button-link-has-colored-icon" type="button" aria-expanded="true" data-aa-button="sd:product:journal:article:location=recommended-articles:type=close"><span class="button-link-text-container"><span class="button-link-text"><h2 class="section-title u-h4"><span class="related-content-panel-title-text">Recommended articles</span></h2></span></span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 92 128" height="20" class="icon icon-navigate-down"><path d="M1 51l7-7 38 38 38-38 7 7-45 45z"></path></svg></button></header><div class="" aria-hidden="false" aria-describedby="recommended-articles-header"><div id="recommended-articles" class="text-xs"><ul><li class="RelatedContentPanelItem u-display-block"><d